{"causes":["The publication of Anna Karenina in 1877 created a cultural stir among Russian literary circles, with many critics praising Tolstoy's innovative narrative structure and psychological insight.","The decline of the Russian Empire during the late 19th century provided a backdrop for Tolstoy to explore themes of social change and individual struggle, resonating deeply with readers facing similar challenges in their own lives.","Tolstoy's personal experiences as a soldier and his interest in social reform influenced his exploration of class and social status in Anna Karenina."],"effects":["The novel's exploration of themes such as love, morality, and social status helped shape the literary canon, influencing authors such as Virginia Woolf and James Joyce.","Anna Karenina's portrayal of a strong-willed female protagonist challenged traditional gender roles, paving the way for feminist literature and inspiring generations of readers.","The novel's impact on Russian culture extends beyond literature, with Anna Karenina becoming an iconic symbol of tragic love and social constraint."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"leo-tolstoy","sourceName":"Leo Tolstoy","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Tolstoy created Anna Karenina as a novel."},{"sourceSlug":"russian-empire","sourceName":"Russian Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"The decline of the Russian Empire during the late 19th century provided a backdrop for Tolstoy to explore themes of social change and individual struggle."},{"sourceSlug":"social-reform","sourceName":"Social Reform","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Tolstoy's personal experiences as a soldier and his interest in social reform influenced his exploration of class and social status in Anna Karenina."},{"sourceSlug":"virginia-woolf","sourceName":"Virginia Woolf","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Woolf was influenced by Anna Karenina's exploration of themes such as love, morality, and social status."},{"sourceSlug":"james-joyce","sourceName":"James Joyce","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Joyce built upon the literary legacy of Anna Karenina, exploring similar themes in his own works."},{"sourceSlug":"russian-literature","sourceName":"Russian Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Anna Karenina is part of the Russian literary canon, influencing generations of readers and authors."},{"sourceSlug":"feminist-literature","sourceName":"Feminist Literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Anna Karenina challenges traditional gender roles, paving the way for feminist literature."},{"sourceSlug":" russian-culture","sourceName":"Russian Culture","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Anna Karenina has influence on Russian culture, becoming an iconic symbol of tragic love and social constraint."},{"sourceSlug":"tragic-love","sourceName":"Tragic Love","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anna-karenina","targetName":"Anna Karenina","context":"Anna Karenina has influence on the concept of tragic love, resonating with readers worldwide."}],"places":[{"name":"St. Petersburg, Russia","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Moscow, Russia","role":"Setting"},{"name":"Tolstoy's country estate, Yasnaya Polyana, Russia","role":"Inspiration"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Anna Karenina","type":"Reference article","slug":"anna-karenina"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q147787","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Karenina"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Anna Karenina left a notable mark on Modern history, documented across 106 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Anna Karenina is the novel by Count Lev Nikolayevich Tolstoy (1828–1910), published serially in the Russian Messenger (Russky Vestnik) from 1875 to 1877 and as a book in 1878 — widely considered one of the greatest novels ever written and the supreme achievement of the realist novel tradition. The novel opens with one of the most famous sentences in world literature: 'All happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way' — a maxim that encapsulates the novel's central concern with the varieties of human unhappiness, desire, and social constraint. The narrative follows two interweaved storylines: the passionate, doomed affair of Anna Karenina (a beautiful, intelligent married aristocrat) with the cavalry officer Count Vronsky, which ends in Anna's suicide under a train; and the contrasting story of Konstantin Levin, a landowner struggling with questions of faith, love, agricultural reform, and the meaning of life, who finds happiness in marriage to Kitty and in his work on the land.

Anna Karenina is simultaneously a society novel, a psychological novel, and a philosophical novel — Tolstoy's presentation of Anna's inner life (her growing paranoia, jealousy, and alienation as her social position deteriorates and her relationship with Vronsky begins to fail) is among the most penetrating psychological analyses in fiction; and the Levin subplot is a barely disguised philosophical autobiography in which Tolstoy works through his own crisis of faith and his rejection of secular aristocratic culture. The two plots are linked by Tolstoy's moral judgement on Anna's transgression — a judgement that has been contested by readers and critics since the novel's publication — and by the epigraph from Romans 12:19: 'Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.'

Anna Karenina has been adapted for film more than any other classic novel — approximately 30 film and television adaptations exist, including Greta Garbo (1935), Vivien Leigh (1948), Sophie Marceau (1997), and Keira Knightley (2012) — and its heroine has been one of the most discussed figures in the feminist criticism of the literary canon, as critics debate whether Tolstoy condemns or sympathises with Anna's transgression of social convention.","subjects":["Russian Literature","Modern Era","Leo Tolstoy","Realist Novel","19th Century","Russian History","Adultery in Literature","Women in Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Anna Karenina's exploration of themes such as love, morality, and social status helped shape the literary canon and challenge traditional gender roles, making it a significant work in Russian literature.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
